course: biology
lesson: 32
title: Frogs, toads, newts and salamanders
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Frogs, toads, newts and salamanders are amphibians, which means «double life»: most start life in water and later live partly on land.
explain: Look at a clump of frogspawn in a garden pond in March: each black dot is an egg in a ball of jelly, and in a few months it becomes a tadpole with gills, then grows back legs, front legs, and soaks its tail back into its body.
explain: Amphibian skin is thin and wet; frogs breathe partly through it and even drink through it, so they must stay damp and are hurt fast by dirty water.
explain: Some are record breakers: a wood frog freezes solid in the Alaskan winter and hops away in spring, and a Goliath frog from Cameroon is as big as a cat.
explain: Many old stories about them are wrong: toads do not give you warts, and salamanders are not born in fire, they just ran out of damp logs thrown on the fire.
fact: A common frog lays about 1,000 to 2,000 eggs in one clump of frogspawn, while toads lay their eggs in long strings round water plants; most eggs and tadpoles are eaten, so only a handful grow up, and adult frogs hunt on land and come back to the pond to breed.
fact: Tadpoles breathe with gills; during metamorphosis they grow back legs, then front legs, form lungs, and the body breaks down the tail and uses it as food.
fact: Frogs breathe partly through their thin, moist skin and drink through their skin, so they need damp places and are harmed quickly by pollution.
fact: Toads have dry, warty skin and short legs for walking; on wet spring nights they walk back to the pond where they hatched, and toad patrols carry them across roads.
fact: Touching a toad does not give you warts, which are caused by a human virus, but toads have a bitter poison behind their eyes, so wash your hands.
fact: A frog swallows prey whole and pulls its eyeballs down into its head to help push the food down its throat.
fact: Poison dart frogs warn enemies with bright colours; they get their poison from ants and mites they eat in the wild, so zoo frogs fed on fruit flies are not poisonous.
fact: Newts are amphibians with tails; in spring the male great crested newt grows a tall wavy crest, and newts can regrow lost legs and tails.
fact: The axolotl is a salamander that keeps its feathery baby gills and stays in water all its life; wild axolotls live only in a few canals near Mexico City and are critically endangered.
fact: Fire salamanders are black and yellow to warn that their skin poison tastes awful; the old belief that they live in fire is a myth.
fact: The wood frog lets about two thirds of the water in its body freeze; its heart stops, and sugar works as antifreeze inside its cells until it thaws in spring; doctors study it to learn how to keep organs for transplant, body parts given to sick people, cold for longer.
fact: Male frogs call to rivals and females, often blowing up a vocal sac that makes the sound louder, like the body of a guitar.
fact: The Goliath frog of West Africa can be about 32 cm long and weigh over 3 kg, while the smallest frogs are under 8 mm long.
exercise: What is a clump of frogspawn? | Hundreds of frog eggs, each in a ball of jelly | A kind of pond weed with little black seeds in it | Frog droppings floating on the water | A nest of baby fish
exercise: About how many eggs are in one clump of common frog spawn? | About 1,000 to 2,000 | About 10 | About 50 | About a million, too many to count
exercise: How do toads lay their eggs? | In long strings wound round water plants | In a round clump like frogs | One by one, each folded inside a leaf under water | In holes on dry land
exercise: Tadpoles in a classroom tank change. Which legs appear first? | The back legs | The front legs | All four at once | They grow no legs, only fins
exercise: What happens to a tadpole's tail when it becomes a frog? | The body breaks it down and uses it as food | It falls off and floats away | It turns into the back legs | It slowly shrinks and becomes the frog's long tongue
exercise: Why must a frog's skin stay moist? | It breathes partly through its skin, which only works when wet | Dry skin makes it far too heavy to jump away from danger | It is the only way to stay warm | Wet skin keeps away all birds
exercise: Why do toads cross roads on rainy March nights? | To walk back to the pond where they hatched to lay eggs | To find warmer roads to sleep on | To escape from ponds that dry up in spring and find bigger lakes | To hunt cars' headlights for insects
exercise: Why does a frog blink hard when it swallows a fly? | It pulls its eyeballs down to push the food down its throat | It is scared of the taste of flies | It is cleaning the backs of its eyes with the tip of its tongue | It is counting how many flies it ate
exercise: Why is a poison dart frog so brightly coloured? | To warn birds and snakes that it is poisonous | To attract insects to eat | To hide among the red and blue flowers of the forest | Because it eats coloured sweets
exercise: Where do wild poison dart frogs get their poison? | From the ants and mites they eat | They make it themselves from sunlight on their skin | From the water they swim in | From biting other frogs
exercise: What does a male great crested newt grow in spring? | A tall wavy crest along its back and tail | A pair of small wings | A hard shell on its back | Long whiskers on its face, like a cat's
exercise: What is special about an axolotl? | It keeps its feathery baby gills and stays in water all its life | It turns into a frog when it is one year old, then leaves the water | It lives only on dry land in deserts | It has fur to keep it warm
exercise: Where do wild axolotls live? | Only in a few canals near Mexico City | In almost every pond and lake in Europe | In the deep sea off Japan | In the rivers of Australia
exercise: Why is the fire salamander black and yellow? | To warn that its skin poison tastes awful | Because it was burnt in a fire | To hide among yellow flowers | Because it is a kind of big bee with no wings
exercise: How does a wood frog survive being frozen solid? | Sugar in its cells works like antifreeze and protects them | It keeps a small fire burning in its stomach to stay warm all winter | Its heart keeps beating fast all winter | It is never really frozen, it just pretends
exercise: Why do so many frogs croak loudly on a warm spring night? | Males are calling to rivals and females | They are warning one another that it will rain tomorrow | They are scared of the dark | They are coughing up flies
exercise: What makes a frog's croak so loud? | A vocal sac that blows up like a balloon | A long tube in its tail | Its teeth clicking together | Its thin skin vibrating fast like a drum
exercise: How big can a Goliath frog be? | About 32 cm long and over 3 kg, as big as a cat | About the size of a fingernail | As big as a horse | About 2 cm long, the size of a pea or a fingernail
test: bruv, dont touch that toad, you'll get warts. true? | No, warts come from a human virus, but wash your hands after | Yes, every touch gives you a new wart on your hands | Only if you touch it on a Friday | Yes, but only on your feet
test: In a cartoon a salamander is born in a campfire and dances in the flames. How did that idea really start? | Salamanders hiding in damp logs ran out when the logs were burnt | Salamanders really are fireproof | Salamanders breathe fire like dragons | People saw salamanders eating hot coals from the fire, so they believed it
test: A pond is full of frogs and frogspawn every spring. What does that tell you about the pond? | Its water is probably clean | It is badly polluted | It has no insects at all | It must be very salty
test: What would happen if a frog were kept in a dry box with no water for days? | Its skin would dry and it could not breathe or drink properly | Nothing at all, as frogs only need water when they are tadpoles | It would grow a hard shell | It would turn back into a tadpole
test: A zoo keeper picks up a poison dart frog bred in the zoo. Why is she not worried? | Zoo frogs eat fruit flies, not the ants that make them poisonous | Zoo frogs are painted with safe colours over their real poison | The poison only works at night | Keepers are immune to all frogs
test: A frog from 1,000 eggs: how many will usually grow into adult frogs? | Only a handful, as most are eaten | Almost all of them | Exactly half | About a hundred, every time
test: i read the tadpoles tail just drops off like a lizard's lol. right? | No, the body breaks the tail down and uses it as food | Yes, it drops off on the pond floor | Yes, and another tadpole eats it | No, the tail slowly becomes the frog's long sticky tongue
test: In a game your axolotl pet grows up into a lizard and walks out of the tank. What really happens to axolotls? | They keep their gills and stay in water all their lives | They all turn into frogs | They grow wings at age two | They become fish when they are grown up
test: Why are scientists interested in wood frogs for hospitals? | To learn how to keep organs for transplant cold for longer | To make new medicines for coughs | To breed frogs for food | To learn how to make sick people hibernate through the summer months
test: Toads and frogs both live near ponds. How can you tell a toad? | Dry, warty skin and short legs for walking | Smooth wet skin and long legs for jumping | A long tail and a wavy crest | Feathery gills on its head
test: A newt loses its tail to a heron's beak but escapes. What will probably happen? | It can grow a new tail | It will die without its tail | It will turn into a frog | It will grow a second head
test: Why would a common frog leave its pond after spring and live under logs? | Adult frogs hunt on land and only need the pond to breed | Ponds are too cold in summer for any animal to live in them | Fish chase all frogs out of ponds | Frogs cannot swim once they are adults
exam: Identify the principal reason for which amphibians are especially susceptible to pollution of their habitat. | Their thin, moist skin readily absorbs substances | They possess no lungs at any stage | They feed exclusively upon pollutants found in the water | They are unable to leave the water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the fate of the tadpole's tail during metamorphosis? | It is broken down and its material reused by the body | It is shed and abandoned upon the pond floor | It is converted into the hind limbs | It persists into adulthood unchanged, as with the newt
exam: Ascertain the source of toxicity in wild poison dart frogs. | Their diet of ants and mites | The water of their habitat | Exposure to sunlight | Their bright pigmentation
exam: It is asserted that contact with a toad occasions warts. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false, warts being caused by a human virus | The assertion is true in all cases | The assertion is true for children only | The assertion is true only of the common toad and not of the frog
